<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#bead">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bead</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as beads</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beads are small decorative objects of glass, stone, shell or metal, pierced for threading, which served widely as trade goods and stores of value. Sub-Saharan beads found at Garamantian sites illustrate the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a>'s trans-Saharan exchange. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, imported beads were traded for gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> at coastal centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi</a> empires to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> commerce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bead.jpg" alt="Bead" width="289" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pschemp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beads.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
